            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal />
                <mainWalls />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 178 litre electric hot water tank, that was manufactured almost 8 years ago. Most electric hot water tanks in Yellowknife are lucky to last 8 years, so you may want to consider alternatives for replacement. We discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.92 EF, could potentially save $ 1,188 per year in energy costs,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 84% efficient oil furnace, vented up a 6" chimney and there is no dedicated outside combustion air supply. This means your furnace is drawing its' combustion air from inside the house, which takes warm interior air up the chimney &amp; out of your house. Your house is also quite air tight now after the renovations, which may create an issue where combustion gases may be backdrafted into your house (see the Additional Comments section of this report).



I modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This type of furnace will no longer require the existing furnace chimney, so it can be removed completely. And ......... it draws its' combustion air directly from outside.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 640 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 3 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front facing roof (East), directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 2,324 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 767, and qualify for $3,900 from the Canada Greener Homes program.

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



As there are no exhaust appliances that vent to the outside (see next section), I was not able to perform a depressurization test (which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside) to see how much negative pressure they can generate inside your house. Whenever your exhaust fans can create a negative pressure of 5+ Pascals, the use of this equipment could draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace is chimney vented and I did not see a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe, so it could be susceptible to back-drafting combustion gases into the house.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed some older smoke detectors, however did not see a carbon monoxide (CO) detector. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has no exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside

        - There are 3 x bathroom fans and none of them are working

        - There is another exhaust fan in the upper laundry room, that is also not working.

        - There are 2 x "ventless" clothes dryers

        - There is a 4" fresh air supply duct connected to the return plenum of the furnace.



Your house is actually a little under-ventilated and would benefit from having the bathroom fans replaced with new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. You may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so you can turn them on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat. The air exhausted by these bathroom fans will be replaced with outside fresh air via the 4" supply duct connected to the return plenum of the furnace.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.40 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: ken.baigent@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!



